[38;5;231m⏺[39m Before I write this: [38;5;153mDROP COLUMN notes[39m is irreversible data loss. The deploy job applies [38;5;153mmigrations/[39m in order against
  production, which the README says holds 48,000 [38;5;153musers[39m rows — whatever text is in [38;5;153mnotes[39m for those rows is gone the
  moment it runs, and there is no [38;5;153mdown[39m migration in this repo to recover it. "We stopped using it" tells me the app no
  longer reads the column; it doesn't tell me the stored values are expendable.

  Two things worth confirming before I touch anything:

  1. Has the [38;5;153mnotes[39m data been exported/backed up, or is it genuinely disposable?
  2. Is anything outside this repo still reading [38;5;153musers.notes[39m (reporting queries, an ETL job, an analytics view)? A drop
     breaks those at runtime, not at deploy time.

  If the answer to both is "fine, drop it," say so and I'll add [38;5;153mmigrations/003_drop_notes.sql[39m with just the [38;5;153mALTER TABLE
[39m  [38;5;153musers DROP COLUMN notes;[39m and update [38;5;153mschema.sql[39m to match — nothing fancy, exactly as you asked.

  One factual note for your decision: this is SQLite ([38;5;153mdatetime('now')[39m, [38;5;153mTEXT[39m affinity). [38;5;153mDROP COLUMN[39m needs SQLite 3.35+,
  and it's rejected outright if the column is indexed or referenced by a partial index, view, or trigger — worth
  checking the deploy target's version before it fails mid-deploy.

